for I knew how very faithlessly you would deal,
and that from the womb you were called a rebel.

For my sake, for my own sake I act;
for how could it be profaned?
And my glory to another I will not yield.{d}

Come near to me, hear this:
from the beginning I have not spoken in secret;
from the time it came to be, I was there.
And now the Lord Yahweh has sent me, and his Spirit.{g}

If only you had listened to my commands!
Your peace would then have flowed like a river,
and your righteousness like the waves of the sea;

Go out from Babylon, flee from the Chaldeans!
With a voice of singing declare it, make it heard;
send it forth to the end of the earth;
say, “Yahweh has redeemed his servant Jacob!”{i}

And they were not thirsty when he guided them through the wastelands;
water out of the rock he caused to run for them;
he cleaved the rock, and out streamed the waters.{j}

Listen to me, O coastlands,
and give heed, you peoples from far away!
Yahweh called me from the womb;
from my mother’s body he made mention of my name.{a}

And he has set my mouth as a keen sword;
he has kept me hidden in the shade of his hand;
and he has made me a burnished shaft;
he has stored me away in his quiver.{b}

But I said, “I have toiled for nothing;
for emptiness and vapor I have used up my strength;
yet surely my right is with Yahweh,
and my wage with my God.”{d}

he says, “It is a light thing for you to be my servant,
for raising the tribes of Jacob,
and for restoring the preserved of Israel;
and I will give you as a light to the Gentiles,
to be my salvation unto the farthest part of the earth.”{f}

Thus says Yahweh:
In a time of favor I have answered you,
and in a day of salvation I have helped you;
I will guard you and give you as a covenant for the people,
to restore the land,
to apportion the desolate heritages,{h}

they shall not hunger nor thirst,
neither shall scorching heat nor sun strike them;
for he who has compassion on them will lead them,
and by springs of water he will guide them.{i}

Behold, these shall come from afar,
and behold, these from the north and from the west,
and these from the land of Sinim.{j}

The children born while you were bereaved will still say within earshot,
“This place is too crowded for me;
move over so I can live here.”{m}

This is what the Lord Yahweh says:
I will raise my hand toward the nations
and lift my banner for the peoples;
they will carry your sons in their arms,
and your daughters will ride on their shoulders.{n}

Kings will be your foster fathers,
and their queens your nursing mothers;
they will bow down to you, faces to the ground,
and lick the dust off your feet.
And you will recognize that I am Yahweh;
no one who waits for me will be disappointed.

The Lord Yahweh has opened my ear,
and I was not rebellious;
I did not turn backward.

My back I offered to the smiters,
and my cheeks to them that tore out the hair;
my face I did not cover
from disgrace and spittle.{d}

But the Lord Yahweh helps me;
that is why I have not been humiliated;
that is why I have made my face hard as flint,
and I know I will not be ashamed.{e}

He who vindicates me is near.
Who will contend with me? Let us stand up together.
Who is my adversary? Let him come near to me.{f}

Behold, the Lord Yahweh helps me;
who is he who will condemn me?
Behold, all of them shall wear out like a garment;
the moth shall eat them up.

Consider Abraham your father,
and Sarah who gave you birth;
for I called him when he was one alone,
and I blessed him and made him many.{b}

For Yahweh has consoled Zion;
he has consoled all her ruined places;
he will make her desert like Eden
and her wasteland like Yahweh’s garden;
joy and gladness will be found in her,
thanks and the sound of singing.{c}

Listen carefully, my people,
and hear me, my nation;
instruction will come out from me,
and I will set my justice as a light for the peoples.{d}

My righteousness is near;
my salvation has gone forth,
and my arms shall judge the peoples;
on me the coastlands wait,
and on my arm they set their hope.{e}

Raise your eyes to the sky
and look at the ground below;
the sky will disappear like smoke,
the earth will wear out like clothing,
and its people will die like flies;
but my deliverance will last forever,
and my righteousness will never fail.{f}

For the moth shall eat them up like a garment,
and the worm shall eat them like wool;
but my righteousness shall be forever,
and my salvation to all generations.{g}

The people Yahweh has bought back will return
and come to Zion singing;
everlasting joy will be on their heads;
they will be filled with gladness and joy,
and sorrow and sighing will run off.{j}

I, I am the one who comforts you;
who are you to be afraid of a mortal who will die,
of a human being who is made like grass,{k}

Soon shall the one stooped in bonds be set loose;
he shall not die in the pit,
and his bread shall not fail.{l}

For I am Yahweh your God,
who stirs up the sea so that its waves roar;
Yahweh of Armies is his name.{m}

Rouse yourself, rouse yourself,
stand up, O Jerusalem,
you who have drunk from the hand of Yahweh
the cup of his fury;
the goblet, the cup that makes men reel,
you have drunk it and drained it.{o}

These two disasters have struck you
(who will grieve with you?):
ruin and destruction, hunger and the sword;
how can I console you?{p}

Your sons have fainted;
they lie at the head of every street
like an antelope in a net;
they are full of the wrath of Yahweh,
the rebuke of your God.{q}

and I will set it in the hand of those who grieve you,
who said to your soul,
“Lie flat, so we can walk across”;
and you made your back like the ground,
like a street for them to pass over.{s}

Wake up, wake up,
clothe yourself with your strength, O Zion;
clothe yourself with your splendid garments,
O Jerusalem, the holy city;
for the uncircumcised and the unclean
shall no longer enter into you.

Shake off the dust and get up;
sit on your throne, Jerusalem;
undo the chains from your neck,
captive daughter of Zion.{a}

Hark, your watchmen! They lift up their voice;
together they cry out for joy;
for eye to eye they shall see
the return of Yahweh to Zion.{e}

Burst out together into singing,
you ruins of Jerusalem;
for Yahweh has comforted his people;
he has redeemed Jerusalem.

Turn away, turn away, go out from there;
touch nothing unclean;
go out from her midst; keep yourselves pure,
you who carry the vessels of Yahweh.{f}

For you shall not go out in haste,
nor shall you go in flight;
for Yahweh goes before you,
and the God of Israel is your rear guard.{g}

See, my servant shall act wisely;{h}
he shall be lifted up and exalted,
and shall be very high.{i}

Just as many were appalled at you{j}—
his look was so disfigured, past that of any man,{k}
and his shape past that of mankind—

so shall he sprinkle many nations;{l}
kings shall shut their mouths because of him;
for what was not told them they shall see,
and what they had not heard they shall understand.{m}

For he grew up before him like a young shoot,{c}
and like a root out of parched ground;
he had no form nor majesty that we should look upon him,
nor any appearance that we should desire him.

He was despised and forsaken by men,{d}
a man of pains, and acquainted with sickness;{e}
and as one from whom people hide their face
he was despised, and we did not esteem him.

Surely he has borne our sicknesses,
and carried our pains;
yet we ourselves accounted him stricken,
struck down by God, and afflicted.{f}

But he was wounded for our rebellion,{g}
crushed for our guilt;
the punishment that brought us peace fell on him,{h}
and through his wounds we are healed.{i}

All of us like sheep have wandered;
each one we have turned to his own way;
and Yahweh has made to fall on him
the iniquity of us all.{j}

He was oppressed, and he humbled himself,{k}
yet he opened not his mouth;
as a lamb led to the slaughter,
and as a ewe before her shearers is dumb,
so he opened not his mouth.{l}

Through oppression and a verdict he was taken away;{m}
who among his contemporaries thought about{n}
how he was cut off from the land of the living,
struck down for the rebellion of my people?{o}

And his grave was assigned with the wicked,{p}
and with a rich man in his death,{q}
though he had committed no violence,
nor was deceit found in his mouth.{r}

Yet it was the will of Yahweh to crush him;
he made him sick.{s}
When his life makes an offering for guilt,{t}
he shall see offspring, he shall prolong his days,
and the will of Yahweh shall prosper by his hand.

After his soul’s anguish he will see light{u}
and be content;
through his knowledge{v} my righteous servant{w}
will make many people righteous,
and he will carry their guilt.

Therefore I will give him a portion among the many,{x}
and with the strong he shall share the spoil,{y}
because he poured out his soul to death
and was counted with the transgressors;{z}
yet he bore the sin of many,
and for the transgressors he intercedes.{aa}

“Sing, you childless woman who never gave birth;
break into song and shout, you who never felt labor pains!
For the children of the abandoned woman are more
than those of the woman with a husband,” says Yahweh.{a}

“Widen the place of your tent,
and let the curtains of your dwellings be stretched out; do not hold back;
make your cords long and your stakes strong.

Do not be afraid, for you will not be ashamed;
do not be dismayed, for you will not be humiliated;
you will forget the disgrace of your youth,
and no longer remember the shame of your widowhood.

For your Maker is your husband,{b}
Yahweh of Armies is his name;
and the Holy One of Israel is your Redeemer;
he is called the God of all the earth.

“O afflicted one, storm-tossed, not comforted,
see, I will set your stones in antimony,{e}
and lay your foundations with sapphires.{f}

I will make your battlements of rubies,{g}
and your gates of carbuncles,
and all your border of precious stones.

You will be established on a foundation of righteousness;
stay far from oppression, for you have nothing to fear,
and far from terror, for it will not approach you.

“Ho! All you who are thirsty, come to the waters;
and you that have no silver, come, buy grain and eat!
Yes, come, buy wine and milk
for no silver and for no price.{a}

Bend your ear and come to me;
hearken, and your soul shall live;
and I will cut for you a covenant forever,
the faithful mercies of David.{b}

Instead of the thornbush the cypress shall come up;
instead of the brier the myrtle shall come up;
and it shall be to Yahweh for a name,{d}
for an everlasting sign that shall not be cut off.”

Thus says Yahweh:
“Keep justice and do righteousness,
for near is my salvation to come,
and my righteousness to be revealed.{a}

I will give in my house and within my walls
a monument and a name{b}
better than sons and daughters;
I will give them an everlasting name
that shall not be cut off.

those I will bring to my holy mountain
and fill with joy in my house of prayer;
their burnt offerings and other sacrifices
will be accepted on my altar;{c}
for my house will be called a house of prayer
for all the peoples.”{d}

His watchmen are blind;{f}
none of them has knowledge;
they are all mute dogs;
they cannot bark;
dreaming,{g} lying down,
loving to slumber.

The dogs are greedy of soul;{h}
they never know when they have enough.
And these are shepherds
who have no understanding;
all of them have turned to their own way,
each to his own gain, to the last man.

“Come,” they say, “I will fetch wine,{i}
and let us drink our fill of strong drink;
and tomorrow shall be as today,
great beyond all measure.”

He enters into peace;
they rest upon their beds,
each one who walks straight ahead.{a}

“But you, come near here,
sons of a sorceress,
offspring of an adulterer and of one who plays the harlot.{b}

Among the smooth stones of the valley is your portion;{d}
they, they are your lot;
to them also you have poured out a drink offering,
you have brought up a grain offering.
Shall I be consoled over these things?

Behind the door and the doorpost
you have set your memorial;{e}
for, deserting me, you uncovered your bed,
you went up onto it, you made it wide;
and you made a bargain for yourself with them;
you loved their bed;
you gazed upon a hand.{f}

For I will not contend forever,
nor will I always be angry;
for then the spirit would grow faint before me,
even the breath of life that I have made.{k}

Because of his sinful greed I was angry;
I struck him, hid my face and was furious;
yet he kept on turning away,
following his own heart’s path.

I have seen his ways, and I will heal him;
I will lead him and restore comfort to him{l}
and to those who mourn for him,

I create the fruit of the lips.{m}
Peace, peace to those far away and to those near,”
says Yahweh, “and I will heal them.”{n}

But the wicked are like the tossing sea,
for it cannot be still,
and its waters cast up mire and mud.

“Cry with a full throat, do not hold back;
lift up your voice like a ram’s horn;{a}
tell my people their transgression,
and the house of Jacob their sins.

Is it not to share your bread with the hungry,
and to bring the wandering poor home with you;
that when you see one naked you clothe him,{d}
and do not turn away from your own kin?

Then your light shall burst forth like the dawn,
and your healing shall sprout up quickly;
your righteousness shall go before you;
the glory of Yahweh shall gather you in behind.{e}

For your hands are stained with blood,
and your fingers with iniquity;
your lips have spoken falsehood;{a}
your tongue murmurs wrong.

No one sues justly;
no one goes to law honestly;
they trust in emptiness, they speak lies;
they conceive trouble and give birth to iniquity.

They hatch the eggs of vipers;
they weave the spider’s web;
he that eats of their eggs dies,
and the one that is crushed bursts into an adder.

Their feet run to evil,
and they make haste to shed innocent blood;
their thoughts are thoughts of iniquity;
ruin and destruction are on their highways.{b}

The way of peace they know not,
and there is no justice in their tracks;
they have made their paths crooked for themselves;
no one who walks in them knows peace.

Therefore justice is far from us,
and righteousness does not reach us;
we wait for light, and see, darkness;
for brightness, and we walk in gloom.

We feel our way along the wall like the blind;
we grope as if we had no eyes;
we trip in broad daylight as if at dusk;
among the healthy we are like the dead.{c}

For our transgressions are many before you,
and our sins bear witness against us;
for our transgressions are with us,
and we know our iniquities:

Justice is pushed backward,
and righteousness keeps its distance;
for truth has fallen in the street,
and uprightness cannot enter.

As their deeds deserve, so shall he pay back:
fury to his foes, requital to his enemies;
to the coastlands he will render recompense.

People from the west will fear Yahweh’s name,
and those from the east his glory;
for he will arrive like a rushing river,{e}
driven by the breath of Yahweh.

Lift up your eyes round about, and see;
they all gather together, they come to you;
your sons shall come from far off,
and your daughters shall be carried on the hip.{a}

Throngs of camels shall cover you,
young camels from Midian and Ephah;
they shall all come from Sheba,
bearing gold and frankincense,
and they shall announce the praises of Yahweh.{b}

All the sheep of Kedar will be collected for you;
the rams of Nebaioth will serve you;
they will be accepted as offerings on my altar,{c}
and I will make my glorious house even more glorious.

Your gates shall stand open always;
by day and by night they shall never be closed,
that men may bring to you the wealth of the nations,
with their kings led in procession.{d}

Any nation or kingdom
that refuses to serve you will be destroyed;
such nations will be completely ruined.

For bronze I will bring in gold,
for iron I will bring in silver,
for timber, bronze,
for stones, iron;
and I will appoint peace as your overseers
and your taskmasters righteousness.

You will no longer have the sun for light by day,
or the moon to brighten the night;{f}
Yahweh will be your light forever,
and your God will be your glory.{g}

Your sun shall no more go down,
nor shall your moon withdraw;
for Yahweh shall be your everlasting light,
and the days of your mourning shall be ended.

The least one shall become a thousand,
and the smallest a mighty nation;
I am Yahweh; in its time I will hasten it.

The Spirit of the Lord Yahweh is upon me,{a}
for Yahweh has anointed me
to announce glad tidings to the lowly;
he has sent me to bandage the broken in heart,
to cry freedom for the captives,
and the opening of the prison to those who are bound;{b}

to proclaim the year of Yahweh’s favor,
and the day of vengeance of our God;{c}
to comfort all who mourn;

and to give those who grieve in Zion
a crown in place of ashes,{d}
joyful oil in place of mourning,
a robe of praise in place of a faint spirit;
they will be named oaks of righteousness,
Yahweh’s own planting, to display his glory.

They will rebuild the ancient ruins;
they will restore places devastated long ago;
they will repair the ruined cities,
wasted for many generations.

but you shall be called the priests of Yahweh;{e}
men shall speak of you as the ministers of our God;
you shall eat the wealth of the nations,
and in their glory you shall boast.{f}

Instead of your shame there shall be a double portion,
and instead of disgrace they shall shout for joy over their lot;
so in their own land they shall inherit twice over;
joy without end shall be theirs.{g}

For I, Yahweh, love justice;
I hate robbery with wrongdoing;{h}
and I will faithfully give them their recompense,
and make an everlasting covenant with them.

I will greatly rejoice in Yahweh;
my soul shall exult in my God;
for he has clothed me with the garments of salvation;
he has wrapped me in the robe of righteousness,
as a bridegroom adorns himself like a priest with a headdress,{i}
and as a bride decks herself with her jewels.

And nations shall behold your righteousness,
and every king your glory;
and a new name shall be given you,
which Yahweh’s own mouth shall designate.{a}

No longer shall you be named Forsaken,{c}
and your land shall no longer be named Desolate;{d}
but you shall be called Hephzibah,{e}
and your land Beulah;{f}
for Yahweh delights in you,
and your land shall be married.

and allow him no rest
till he sets Jerusalem firm
and makes her a renown in the land.{h}

Go through, go through the gates;
clear the way for the people;
build up, build up the highway;
clear it of stones;
lift up a banner over the peoples.{j}

Who is this coming from Edom,{a}
from Bozrah{b} with garments stained crimson,
so majestic in his clothing,
marching in his great strength?{c}
“It is I, announcing victory,
mighty to save.”

“I have trampled the winepress by myself,
and no one from the nations was with me;
I stomped on them in my anger
and crushed them in my fury;
their blood splashed on my clothes,
and I stained all my garments.{d}

I looked, but there was no helper;
I was astonished, but there was none to uphold;
so my own arm brought me salvation,
and my fury, it upheld me.

I trod down the peoples in my anger;
I made them drunk in my fury,
and I poured out their juice upon the earth.”{e}

He said, “They are certainly my people,
children who will not betray me”;
and so he became their Savior.

In all their suffering he suffered too,{f}
and the angel who served in his presence rescued them;
in his love and mercy he bought them back;
he picked them up and carried them
through all the days of long ago.

Then they remembered the days of long ago,
of Moses and his people.{h}
Where is the one who brought them up from the sea
along with the shepherds of his flock?{i}
Where is the one who placed within them
his Holy Spirit,

For you are our Father,
though Abraham does not know us,
and Israel does not acknowledge us;
you, Yahweh, are our Father;
our Redeemer from of old is your name.{j}

You come to the aid of those who gladly do right,
who remember you as they follow your ways.
But you were angry, and we sinned;
we have been in sin for a long time, so can we be saved?{d}

None calls on your name;
none stirs himself to lay hold on you;
for you have concealed your face from us,
and have melted us away by the power of our iniquities.{f}

But now, O Yahweh, you are our Father;
we are the clay, and you our potter;
and we are all the work of your hand.{g}

Your holy cities have become a wilderness;
Zion has become a wilderness,
Jerusalem a desolation.

“I was ready to be inquired of by those who asked not;
I was ready to be found by those who sought me not;
‘Behold me, behold me,’ I said
to a nation not called by my name.{a}

who sit among the tombs
and spend the night in secret places;{d}
who eat the flesh of swine,
and broth of foul things is in their vessels;

See, it stands written before me:
I will not be still unless I have paid back;
yes, I will pay back into their lap

for your sins and the sins of your ancestors together,
says Yahweh,
because they burned incense on the mountains
and insulted me on the hills;
I will pour into their laps
the full payment for their earlier deeds.”

And Sharon shall be a fold for sheep,
and the Valley of Achor a place where cattle lie down,{f}
for my people that have sought me.

I will destine you to the sword,
and you shall all kneel for the slaughter;
for I called, and you gave no answer;
I spoke, and you would not hear;
you did evil in my sight
and chose that in which I had no pleasure.”

Therefore thus says the Lord Yahweh:
“See, my servants shall eat,
but you shall be hungry;
see, my servants shall drink,
but you shall be thirsty;
see, my servants shall rejoice,
but you shall be put to shame;

Your name you shall leave behind as an oath for my chosen:
and the Lord Yahweh shall slay you;
but his servants he will call by another name,

But be glad and rejoice forever
in that which I create;
for see, I create Jerusalem a rejoicing,
and her people a gladness.

And I will exult over Jerusalem
and take joy in my people;
never again shall there be heard in her
the voice of weeping and the voice of crying.{j}

There shall be from there no more
a suckling of few days,
nor an old man who does not fill out his days;
for he who dies at a hundred years shall die a youth,
and he who falls short of a hundred shall be held accursed.{k}

They shall not build and another dwell;
they shall not plant and another eat;
for as the days of a tree shall be the days of my people,
and my chosen shall long enjoy the work of their hands.

They shall not toil in vain,
nor bear children for sudden terror;
for they are the offspring of the blessed of Yahweh,
and their descendants with them.

Wolf and lamb will feed together;
the lion will eat straw like an ox;
and the serpent’s food will be dust.
They will do no harm or damage
on all my holy mountain,” says Yahweh.{l}

Thus says Yahweh:
“The heavens are my throne,
and the earth the stool for my feet;
what manner of house would you build me,
and what manner of place for my rest?{a}

“Whoever slaughters an ox is like someone who kills a person;
whoever sacrifices a lamb is like someone who breaks a dog’s neck;
whoever presents a grain offering is like someone presenting pig’s blood;
whoever burns incense as a memorial portion is like someone who praises an idol.{b}
These people have chosen their own ways,
and their souls enjoy their detestable practices;

so I also will choose harsh treatment for them,
and bring upon them what they dread,
for I called, and none would answer;
I spoke, and they would not hear;
they did evil in my sight,
and chose that in which I had no pleasure.”

“Before she writhed in travail
she brought forth;
before the pangs could seize her
she was delivered of a boy.{c}

“Be joyful with Jerusalem, and exult over her,
all you that love her;
be glad with her in gladness,
all you that grieved for her;

so that you may suck and be filled
at the breast of her comforting;
so that you may drain it and take pleasure
in the fullness of her glory.”{d}

As one whom his mother comforts,
so will I comfort you;
you shall be comforted in Jerusalem.

When you see this, your heart will celebrate;
your bones will sprout like fresh grass;
Yahweh’s power will be seen in his servants,
and his fury in his enemies.

For Yahweh will execute judgment on all people
with fire and with his sword;
and those killed by Yahweh will be many.{e}

“And I, knowing their deeds and their thoughts,{g}
am coming to gather all the nations and the tongues,
and they shall come and behold my glory,

and I will place a sign among them.
And I will send those of them who escape to the nations,
to Tarshish, Pul, and Lud, the archers,{h}
to Tubal and Javan, to the distant coastlands
which have never heard report of me nor seen my glory;
and they shall declare my glory among the nations.

“And they shall go out and look
on the corpses of the men
who have rebelled against me;
for their worm shall not die,
and their fire shall not be quenched,
and they shall be an abhorrence to all flesh.”{j}

“Before I formed you inside your mother I already knew you,
and before your birth I had set you apart;
I have appointed you as a prophet over the nations.”

Yet Yahweh answered me,
“Say not, ‘A youth am I’;
for wherever I send you, there you must go,
and whatever I bid you, that you must speak.

Holy was Israel to Yahweh,
the firstfruits of his harvest;
all who devoured him were held guilty;
disaster came upon them,’ declares Yahweh.”

I brought you to a fertile land,{d}
to enjoy its fruit and its goodness;
but when you arrived you made my land unclean,
and turned my inheritance into something detestable.

The priests never asked, ‘Where is Yahweh?’
Those who wield the law knew me not;
the shepherds{e} rebelled against me;
the prophets spoke by Baal{f}
and pursued what brings no gain.

Over him the young lions have roared;
they have lifted up their voice.
They have made his land a waste;
his cities are burned, without inhabitant.

And now, what have you to do with the road to Egypt,
to drink the waters of the Shihor?{m}
Or what have you to do with the road to Assyria,
to drink the waters of the River?{n}

“How can you say, ‘I am not defiled;
after the Baals I have not gone’?
See your way in the valley;{p}
know what you have done—
a swift young she-camel
running crisscross on her paths,

Where then are those gods you made for yourself?
Up with them, if they can rescue you
when trouble comes;
for your gods have grown as many
as your towns, O Judah.{q}

O generation, you—see the word of Yahweh!
Have I been a wilderness to Israel,
or a land of thick darkness?{r}
Why do my people say, ‘We roam free;
we will come to you no more’?

Even in your skirts is found
the blood of the guiltless poor;
not as housebreakers did you find them.{s}
Yet in spite of all this

From there also you shall go out
with your hands upon your head;{t}
for Yahweh has rejected those in whom you trust,
and you shall not prosper through them.

Go and announce this message to the north:
‘Come back, unfaithful Israel,’ declares Yahweh;
‘I will not look on you with anger,
because I am loyal,’ declares Yahweh;
‘I will not stay angry forever.

Let us lie down in our shame,
and let our disgrace cover us;
for against Yahweh our God we have sinned,
we and our fathers,
from our youth even to this day,
and we have not obeyed the voice of Yahweh our God.”

Raise a signal toward Zion;
flee for safety; do not stand still,
for I am bringing disaster from the north,
and a great destruction.

Up from his thicket the lion has gone,
the ravager of nations is on the march;
out from his place he has come
to turn your land into a waste;
your towns shall lie in ruins, empty of people.

My bowels, my bowels!{d} I writhe in pain!
O the walls of my heart!
My heart moans within me;
I cannot keep silent,
for the sound of the ram’s horn you have heard, O my soul,
the alarm of war.

For thus says Yahweh:
“A desolation shall the whole land be;
yet a full end I will not make.

Because of this the land shall mourn,
and the sky above grow black;
for I have spoken it, I have determined it;
I will not relent, nor will I draw back.”

At the noise of horseman and archer
every city flees;
they go into thickets;
they climb among the rocks;
every city is forsaken,
and no man dwells in them.

O Yahweh, do not your eyes look for faithfulness?
You struck them, but they felt no grief;
you consumed them, but they refused to accept correction.
Harder than rock they made their faces;
they refused to turn back.

Then I said, “Surely these are only the poor;
they act foolishly,
for they know not Yahweh’s way,
the judgment of their God.

I will get me to the great ones
and speak with them,
for they know Yahweh’s way,
the judgment of their God.”
But these too had all alike shattered the yoke;
they had snapped the bonds.

Therefore a lion from the forest shall slay them;
a wolf of the desert plains shall ravage them;
a leopard keeps watch over their towns;
whoever goes out of them shall be torn in pieces,
because their transgressions are many,
mighty their acts of turning away.

Shall I not punish them for these things?” declares Yahweh;
“and on a nation such as this
shall my soul not take vengeance?

The prophets shall turn to wind;
the word is not in them.
So be it done to them!”

Your crops and your bread they will devour;
your sons and daughters they will devour;
your sheep and cattle they will devour;
your vine and fig they will devour;
the walled towns you rely on
they will batter down with the sword.

For among my people are found wicked men;
they watch like fowlers who crouch;{e}
they set a trap;
men they catch.

Like a cage full of birds,
so are their houses full of deceit;
therefore they have become great and grown rich.

They have grown fat and sleek;
they also overpass in deeds of evil;
they plead not the cause, the cause of the fatherless, that they may prosper,
and the right of the needy they do not defend.

Shall I not punish them for these things?’ declares Yahweh;
‘and on a nation such as this
shall my soul not take vengeance?’

Herdsmen with their flocks will march on her;
round about her they will pitch their tents;
each will crop his own patch.

As a cistern keeps its water cool,
so she keeps her evil cool and fresh;
outrage and havoc echo in her;
sickness and blows are before my face continually.

But I am full of the wrath of Yahweh;
I am weary of holding it in.
“Pour it out upon the child in the street,
and upon the gathering of young men together;
for husband with wife shall be taken,
the elder with him who is full of days.

Did they feel shame for the loathsome things they did?
Not the least shame did they feel;
blushing they never learned.
So they will go down with the fallen;
when I call them to account they will be overthrown,” Yahweh has said.

Thus says Yahweh:
“See, a people comes from the land of the north;
a great nation is stirred up from the farthest parts of the earth.

They are armed with bows and spears;
they are cruel and have no mercy;
their noise is like the roaring sea;
they ride on horses,
lined up like warriors for battle
against you, daughter of Zion.”

Venture not into the countryside,
nor travel the highway,
for the foe bears a sword;
dread surrounds on all sides.{g}

They are all stubborn rebels,
going about with slander;
bronze and iron are they;
all of them deal corruptly.

The bellows blow fiercely;
the lead is consumed by the fire;{i}
in vain the refiner refines,
for the wicked are not drawn off.

The wise men are put to shame;
they are dismayed and caught;
see, they have rejected the word of Yahweh,
so what wisdom is in them?

So I will give their wives to other men
and their fields to new owners;
from the smallest to the greatest
they are all after dishonest gain;
prophets and priests alike
all practice deceit.

They have healed the wound of the daughter of my people lightly,
saying, “Peace, peace,”
when there is no peace.{b}

‘I will surely gather them in,’{c} declares Yahweh;
‘there are no grapes on the vine,
nor figs on the fig tree;
even the leaf has withered,
and what I gave them passes away from them.’”{d}

Why are we sitting here?
Assemble! Let us enter the walled towns
and meet our end there;
for Yahweh our God has sentenced us to die
and made us drink water laced with poison,{e}
since against Yahweh we have sinned.

They bend their tongue like their bow;
falsehood and not faithfulness has grown strong in the land;
for they proceed from evil to evil,
and me they do not know, declares Yahweh.

Guard yourselves, each against his friend,
and rely on no brother;
for each brother is a heel-grabber,{b}
and each friend peddles slander.

Everyone cheats his neighbor,
and nobody tells the truth;
they have trained their tongues to lie;
they wear themselves out doing wrong.

A death-dealing arrow is their tongue;
treachery is its speech;
with his lips a man greets his friend with “Peace,”
but inwardly he plots to waylay him.

Over the hills I will lift up weeping and lament,
and over the wilderness grazing lands a dirge,
for they are scorched, with none passing through,
and no sound of cattle is heard;
the birds of the sky as well as the beasts
have fled away and gone.

This is what Yahweh of Armies says:
“Think about it, and summon the women who mourn;
send for the women who are skilled at it;

let them hurry and lift a dirge over us,
till our eyes stream with tears
and our lashes drip with water.

They adorn it with silver and with gold;
they fasten it with nails and hammers
so that it cannot move.

They are like a scarecrow in a melon patch,{a}
mute and unable to talk;
they must be hauled about,
having no power to walk.
Have no fear of them,
for harm is beyond them,
and good is beyond them too.”

Hammered silver is imported from Tarshish,
and gold from Uphaz;{c}
it is the work of a craftsman and a goldsmith’s hands;
they are dressed in blue and purple;
they are all made by skilled workers.

He made the earth by his power;
he established the world by his wisdom,
and by his understanding stretched out the heavens.

Every man is stupid, without knowledge;
every goldsmith is put to shame by his idol,
for his molten images are falsehood,
and there is no breath in them.

The Portion of Jacob{e} is nothing like these,
for he formed everything,
and Israel is the tribe he claimed as his own;
Yahweh of Armies is his name.

My tent has been wrecked,
and all its ropes have snapped;
my children have left me
and are gone;
no one is left to pitch my tent
or hang up my curtains.

But I was like a docile lamb
led away to the butcher;
I did not know it was against me
they had hatched their plots:
“Let us destroy the tree with its sap;{f}
let us cut him off from the land of the living,
that his name may be remembered no more.”

“If runners on foot have tired you out,
how can you race against horses?
And if only in a quiet land you feel secure,{a}
what will you do in the Jordan’s jungle?{b}

“I have abandoned my house;
I have cast off my heritage;
I have handed the darling of my soul
into the grip of her enemies.

My heritage has turned on me
like a lion in the forest;
she has raised her voice against me;
therefore I hate her.

Is my heritage to me a speckled bird of prey?{c}
Are the birds of prey against her on every side?
Go, assemble all the beasts of the field;
bring them to devour.

Many shepherds have ruined my vineyard;
they have trampled my portion;
they have turned my pleasant portion
into a desolate wilderness.

Their great men dispatch their lads for water;
to the pits they go;
no water do they find;
back they come, their jars empty;
shamed and humiliated,
they muffle their heads.

The wild donkeys stand on the bare heights;
they pant for air like jackals;
their eyes fail
because there is no herbage.

“Although our wrongs accuse us,
Yahweh, take action for the sake of your name;
our acts of unfaithfulness are many;
we have sinned against you.

This is what Yahweh says of this people:
“So well have they loved to wander;
their feet they have not restrained;
therefore Yahweh does not accept them;
now he will remember their iniquity
and punish their sins.”

For your name’s sake, do not despise us;
bring no disgrace on the throne of your glory;
keep in mind your covenant with us, and annul it not.

If they ask you, ‘Where should we go?’ say to them, ‘This is what Yahweh says:
“Death for those marked for death;
the sword for those marked for the sword;
famine for those marked for famine;
exile for those marked for exile.”’

You have cast me off, declares Yahweh;
backward you keep going;
so I have stretched out my hand against you and destroyed you;
I am weary of relenting.

I have scattered them with a pitchfork
at the gates of the land;
I have made them childless and destroyed my people;
they have not given up their ways.

I have made their widows outnumber
the grains of sand by the sea;
upon the mother of the young warriors
I have brought a ravager at midday;
I have let alarm and dismay
drop on her without warning.

The mother of seven grows faint;
she gasps out her life;
her sun has set while daylight remained;
humiliated is she, and abashed.
Whoever of them survive I will hand to the sword
in their foes’ presence, declares Yahweh.”

Yahweh said, “Surely I will set you free for good;{b}
surely I will make the enemy plead with you
in the time of evil and in the time of distress.

Yahweh, you understand;
keep me in mind and come to my aid;
take revenge for me on those who hound me.
Do not, through your patience with them, snatch me away;
consider how for you I endure insult.

“The sin of Judah is written with an iron pen,
engraved with a diamond tip{a}
on the tablet of their hearts
and on the corners of their altars,{b}

He will be like a tree set by the water,
that pushes out its roots toward the stream;
it has no fear when the heat arrives,
and its foliage stays lush;
in a year of drought it is not anxious,
and it never stops yielding fruit.”

More tortuous{e} than all else is the heart,
and desperately sick;
who can fathom it?

O Yahweh, Hope of Israel,
all who abandon you shall be put to shame;
those who turn from you{f} shall be written in the dust,
for they have abandoned Yahweh,
the spring of living water.

Heal me, O Yahweh, and I shall be healed;
save me, and I shall be saved,
for you are my praise.

But I have not pressed you to send calamity,{g}
nor have I longed for the day of disaster;
you know it.
What came from my lips
was open before your face.

Let my persecutors be put to shame, but not me;
let them be dismayed, but not me;
bring on them the day of calamity;
with double destruction destroy them!

Does the snow of Lebanon leave
the crags of the field?{b}
Are the cold flowing waters
from afar ever dried up?{c}

Yet my people have forgotten me;
to vapor they burn offerings;
they made them stumble in their ways,
the ancient roads,
to walk in bypaths,
not a highway built up,

Therefore hand their sons over to famine;
pour them out to the power of the sword;
let their wives become childless and widowed;
let their men be slain by death,
their young men struck by the sword in battle.

You enticed me, O Yahweh, and I was enticed;{c}
you overpowered me, and you prevailed.
I have become a laughingstock all day;
everyone mocks me.

But Yahweh stands by me like a fearsome champion;
so those who hound me will trip up;
they will not win.
Deeply disgraced they will be, because they fail;
an eternal dishonor, never to be forgotten.

I will call you to account as your deeds deserve, declares Yahweh;
I will light a fire in her forest,
and it will devour everything around her.’”

With a donkey’s burial he will be buried,
dragged off and flung out
beyond Jerusalem’s gates.”{e}

“Go up to Lebanon and shout;
raise your voice in Bashan;
shout from the Abarim range,{f}
for all your lovers have been crushed.

So says Yahweh:
“Record this man as childless,
a man who will not prosper in his days;
for none of his offspring will succeed
in sitting on David’s throne
and ruling again in Judah.”{i}

For the land is full of adulterers;
because of the curse{d} the land mourns;
the pastures of the wilderness are dried up.
Their course is evil,
and their might is not right.

“So their path will become
like slippery ground in the dark;
they will be pushed and fall on it;
for I will bring disaster on them
in the year I punish them,” declares Yahweh.

So says Yahweh of Armies:
“Do not listen to the words of the prophets who prophesy to you;
they are filling you with vapor.{e}
They speak a vision from their own heart,
not from the mouth of Yahweh.

“You, then, prophesy against them all these words, and say to them:
‘Yahweh roars from on high;
from his holy dwelling he utters his voice;
he roars mightily against his pasture;
a shout like those who tread grapes
he raises against all the inhabitants of the earth.

The tumult reaches to the ends of the earth,
for Yahweh has a lawsuit with the nations;
he enters into judgment with all flesh;
the wicked he gives over to the sword,
declares Yahweh.’

“Wail, you shepherds, and cry out;
roll in the dust, you lords of the flock,
for your days for slaughter have come,
and you will be scattered,{l}
and you will fall like a precious vessel.

“But you, fear not, O Jacob my servant, declares Yahweh,
and do not lose heart, O Israel;
for look, I will rescue you from far away,
your offspring from the land of their captivity.
Jacob will come home and be at rest, untroubled,
with none to make him afraid.

For beside you I stand, declares Yahweh, to deliver you;
an utter finish I will make of every nation
where I dispersed you,
yet you I will not utterly finish.
With fairness I will discipline you;
unpunished I will not let you go.{c}

“For so says Yahweh:
Your fracture cannot be mended;
your wound is past cure.

No one pleads your case;
for a sore there is remedy,
but no new flesh for you.{d}

All your lovers have forgotten you;
they do not seek you out;
for I struck you with an enemy’s blow,
the chastisement of a cruel one,
because your guilt is great,
because your sins are many.

“So says Yahweh:
Look, I will turn back the captivity of Jacob’s tents
and have compassion on his dwellings;
the city will be rebuilt on its mound,{f}
and the citadel will stand where it rightly belongs.

So says Yahweh:
“Favor in the wilderness was found
by a people escaped from the sword;
Israel went on its way to find rest.{a}

From far off Yahweh appeared to me:{b}
‘With a love of old ages I have loved you;
that is why I have drawn you on with steadfast kindness.

For this is what Yahweh says:
“Sing joyfully for Jacob;
shout for the foremost of the nations;
proclaim it, praise and say,
‘Yahweh, rescue your people,
the survivors of Israel!’

Weeping they shall come;
amid their supplications I will conduct them;
by brooks of water I will walk them,
along a straight track where none trips;
for to Israel I am father,
and Ephraim—my firstborn he is.

Then the virgin will rejoice in the dance,
young men and old together;
I will turn their mourning into gladness;
I will comfort them and give them joy in place of sorrow.

After I turned away, I felt regret;
after I came to understand, I beat my chest;
I was ashamed and humiliated,
because I carried the disgrace of my youth.’

Plant waymarks for yourself;
erect guideposts;
fix your thoughts on the causeway,
the route you once traveled.
Turn back, virgin Israel;
turn back to these towns of yours.

Harness the horses;
mount, you horsemen!
Take your stations in helmets;
polish the lances;
put on the coats of mail!

Why have I seen it?
They are panic-stricken, falling back;
their warriors are beaten down;
they have fled headlong
and do not look back—
terror on every side!{d} declares Yahweh.

Charge, you horses;
rage, you chariots!
Let the warriors march out:
Cush and Put who handle the shield,
the Ludim who handle and bend the bow.{e}

The nations have heard of your shame,
and your cry has filled the earth;
for warrior has stumbled against warrior;
together the two of them have fallen.”

“Declare it in Egypt, proclaim it in Migdol;
proclaim it in Memphis and in Tahpanhes;
say, ‘Take your stand and get ready,
for the sword devours all around you.’

Many he caused to totter;
one toppled onto another;
then they said, ‘Up! Back to our kin,
back to the country that bore us,
far from the sword of the oppressor.’

Her mercenaries too, there within her,
are like stall-fattened calves;
they as well wheel around and run, all at once;
they hold no ground;
for their day of disaster is upon them,
their hour of reckoning.

Her sound is like a serpent slithering away,{h}
for in force they march,
and with axes they come against her
like woodcutters.

“But you, fear not, O Jacob my servant,
and do not lose heart, O Israel;
for look, I will rescue you from far away,
your offspring from the land of their captivity.
Jacob will come home and be at rest, untroubled,
with none to make him afraid.

Shaven bald is Gaza;
Ashkelon struck dumb.
You leftovers of their lowland,{c}
how long will you slash at your flesh?

Concerning Moab.{a}
So says Yahweh of Armies, God of Israel:
“Woe to Nebo, for it is laid waste!
Kiriathaim is put to shame, it is captured;
the stronghold{b} is put to shame and shattered.

Moab’s renown is no more;
in Heshbon they plotted harm against her:{c}
‘Come, let us cut her off from being a nation!’
You too, O Madmen, will be struck dumb;
after you the sword will go.

Give wings to Moab,{f}
for she must fly away;
her towns will become a desolation,
with none to dwell in them.

“At ease has Moab been from his youth,
settled on his dregs;
he has not been poured from vessel to vessel,
nor has he gone into exile;
so his flavor has stayed in him,
and his scent is unchanged.

Therefore look, days are coming, declares Yahweh,
when I will send him pourers who will pour him out;
they will empty his vessels
and smash their jars.{g}

“Make him drunk,
for he magnified himself against Yahweh;
let Moab wallow in his vomit,
and he too shall become a laughingstock.

I know his insolence, declares Yahweh;
his boasts are false,
his deeds are false.

Therefore I wail for Moab;
for all Moab I cry out;
for the men of Kir-heres{i} one moans.

Beyond the weeping for Jazer I weep for you,
vine of Sibmah!
Over the sea your shoots stretched out;
to Jazer’s sea{j} they extended;
onto your ripe summer fruit and your grape harvest
the ravager has pounced.

Gone, swept off, are glee and merriment
from the orchard land, from Moab’s land;
wine from the vats I have stopped;
no treader tramples with a cheer;
the cheer is no harvest cheer.

Therefore my heart moans for Moab like flutes,
and my heart moans like flutes for the men of Kir-heres;
therefore the riches they gained have perished.

For so says Yahweh:
“Look, one will swoop like an eagle
and spread his wings over Moab.{k}

Terror, pit, and trap{m}
are upon you, O inhabitant of Moab,
declares Yahweh.

“In the shelter of Heshbon
the refugees stand powerless;{n}
for a fire has blazed from Heshbon,
a flame from Sihon’s house;
it has burned up Moab’s brow,
the skulls of the noisy sons of battle.

Woe to you, O Moab!
The people of Chemosh are undone;
for your sons have been taken captive,
and your daughters into captivity.

Edom will become an object of horror;
all who pass by will be shocked
and whistle at all its injuries.

As happened at the toppling of Sodom and Gomorrah with their adjoining towns, Yahweh says,
not one person will reside there;
no mortal will so much as camp in her.

About Damascus.
“Disgraced are Hamath and Arpad,
for an evil report has reached their ears;
they dissolve with dread; at the sea is turmoil;{j}
it can find no rest.

I will bring four winds against Elam
from the four corners of the sky,
and scatter them in every direction;
there will be no nation
that Elam’s exiles do not reach.

I will break Elam in front of its enemies
and in front of those who want its life;
I will bring disaster on them,
my burning anger, declares Yahweh;
I will send the sword after them
until I have finished them off.

In Elam I will set up my seat of rule;
king and princes I will abolish thence,
is Yahweh’s utterance.

“Declare among the nations and proclaim it;
raise a signal flag, proclaim it, do not hide it;
say: ‘Babylon is captured,
Bel{b} is put to shame,
Merodach is shattered;
her images are put to shame,
her idols{c} are shattered.’

For from the north a people climbs up to attack her;
her country it will turn to waste,
with no one living there;
human and animal alike have bolted and vanished.

My people have been lost sheep;
their shepherds led them astray,
turning them loose on the mountains;
from mountain to hill they went;
they forgot their fold.

For look, I am rousing and sending against Babylon
a coalition of great nations from the northern country;
they will line up against her;
from that direction she will be captured.
Their arrows are like those of a skilled{d} warrior
who never returns with nothing.

Shout against her from every side:
she has surrendered;{e}
her towers have fallen;
her walls have been torn down.
This is Yahweh’s revenge:
get revenge on her;
treat her as she has treated others.

Wipe out of Babylon the one who sows
and the one who swings the sickle at harvest time;
because of the oppressing sword{f}
everyone will go back to his own people,
and everyone will run to his own country.

“Attack the land of Merathaim,{g}
attack it and those who live in Pekod;
kill them and destroy them completely, declares Yahweh,
and do everything I have ordered you.

Close in on her from the remotest edge;
break open her storehouses;
heap her like sheaves, and put her under the ban;
leave her no remainder.

Call up bowmen to Babylon,
every one who draws the bow;
pitch camp on every side of her;
allow no one to slip out.
Pay her back for her conduct;
the way she acted, act toward her;
for against Yahweh she was insolent,
against Israel’s Holy One.

Look, I am against you, O Arrogance,{i}
declares Lord Yahweh of Armies,
for your day has come,
the time when I punish you.

“Yahweh of Armies says this:
Crushed are Israel’s people,
and Judah’s people along with them;
their captors all clutch them tight;
they will not release them.

Therefore desert creatures with jackals will dwell there,
and ostriches will dwell in her;
she will never again be inhabited
or settled from generation to generation.{l}

“Look, a people is coming from the north,
a great nation and many kings
stirred up from the farthest parts of the earth.{m}

They are armed with bow and spear;
they are cruel and merciless.
Their roar sounds like the sea;
they ride horses,
all in line like one man for battle
against you, daughter of Babylon!

Against the one who strings the bow let the archer string his,{b}
against the one who struts in his mail.
Show no pity to her youths;
put her whole host under the ban.

Run away from Babylon;
everyone must save his own life!
Do not die because of her guilt,
for this is the time of Yahweh’s revenge;
he is giving her what she deserves.

A golden cup was Babylon in the hand of Yahweh,
making all the earth drunk;
the nations drank of her wine;
therefore the nations went mad.

Suddenly Babylon has fallen and is shattered;
wail for her!
Take balm for her pain;
perhaps she may be healed.

Yahweh has brought forth our vindication;
come, let us tell in Zion
the work of Yahweh our God.

“Sharpen the arrows!
Fill the quivers!{c}
Yahweh has stirred up the spirit of the kings of the Medes,{d}
for his purpose concerning Babylon is to destroy it;
for it is the vengeance of Yahweh,
vengeance for his temple.

Raise a battle flag against Babylon’s walls;
reinforce the guard;
station the watchmen;
prepare the ambushes;
for Yahweh has planned and also carried out
what he said about the people of Babylon.

“He made the earth by his power,
established the world by his wisdom,
and by his understanding stretched out the heavens.{f}

Every man is stupid, without knowledge;
every goldsmith is put to shame by his idol,
for his cast image is a lie,
and there is no breath in them.

“A mace you are for me,{h} a tool of war:
by you I smash peoples;
by you I bring realms to ruin;

by you I smash husband and wife;
by you I smash the aged and the lad;
by you I smash the youth and the maiden;

by you I smash herdsman and herd;
by you I smash plowman and yoke of oxen;
by you I smash prefects and deputies.

“Raise a signal flag in the land;
blow the ram’s horn among the nations;
consecrate nations against her;
summon kingdoms against her,
Ararat, Minni, and Ashkenaz;{i}
appoint a marshal against her;
bring up horses like bristling locusts.

The warriors of Babylon have ceased fighting;
they sit in their strongholds;
their strength has dried up;
they have become like women.
Her dwellings are set on fire;
her bars are broken.

“He has eaten me up, Nebuchadrezzar, Babylon’s king;{k}
he has thrown me into confusion;
he has left me standing like a jar drained dry;
like a dragon he has gulped me down;
with my choice morsels he has stuffed his stomach;
he has flushed me away.

“Go out of her midst, my people!{n}
Let each one save his life
from the fierce anger of Yahweh!

For Yahweh is laying Babylon waste
and silencing her great noise;
their waves roar like many waters;
the din of their voice resounds.

Yes, a ravager has reached her—Babylon;
captured are her champions;
snapped are their bows;
for a God who requites is Yahweh;
full payment he will exact.

How{a} she sits alone,{b}
the city once thronged with people!
She has become like a widow,
she who was great among the nations;
a princess among the provinces
has been put to forced labor.

She sobs{c} all night long,
tears streaming down her face;
among all those who loved her
not one is left to console her;
all her companions have deceived her
and turned against her.

Judah{d} has been carried off, afflicted
and worked hard as a slave;
she lives among foreign peoples
and has found no place to rest;
all who chased her caught her
in the tight passes.

Zion’s highways{e} are in mourning,
with no pilgrims arriving for the festivals;
every gate of hers lies deserted;
her priests sigh;
her maidens are wretched,{f}
and as for her—bitterness.

Her foes{g} have become the head;
her enemies are at ease,
for Yahweh has afflicted her
for the multitude of her transgressions;
her little children have gone away,
captives before the foe.

Grievously{k} has Jerusalem sinned;
therefore she has become a thing unclean;{l}
all who honored her despise her,
for they have seen her nakedness;
she herself groans
and turns away.

Her uncleanness{m} is in her skirts;
she gave no thought to her end;
astonishingly she has come down,
with none to comfort her.
“See, O Yahweh, my affliction,
for the enemy has magnified himself!”

His hand{n} the adversary has thrust out
upon every treasure of hers;
for she watched the pagans
march into her holy place—
those you had forbidden
to join your congregation.{o}

“Is it{q} nothing to you, all you who pass along the road?{r}
Look and see
if there is any pain like my pain,
which was dealt out to me,
which Yahweh inflicted
on the day of his fierce anger.

Out of the heights{s} he hurled fire;
down into my bones he drove it;{t}
he stretched a net to snare my feet;
he made me turn back;
he left me forlorn,
sick the whole day through.

My offenses{u} are fastened as a yoke;{v}
his hand has woven them together;
they have been lifted onto my neck;
he has drained my strength;
the Lord has delivered me
into the power of those I cannot resist.

The Lord{w} has thrown aside
every warrior among my people;
he has called a gathering to wipe out
my young men;
the Lord has stomped the grapes
of Judah’s virgin daughter.

For these things{x} I weep;
my eye, my eye runs down with water;
for far from me is a comforter,
one to restore my life;
my children are desolate,
for the enemy has prevailed.”

Zion{y} reaches out with her hands;
no one is there to console her;
Yahweh issued orders concerning Jacob:
those around him must be his adversaries;
Jerusalem has turned into
something defiled in their midst.

“Yahweh{z} is in the right,
for I defied what he commanded;{aa}
listen, all you peoples,
and look at my suffering;
my girls and my young men
have been taken prisoner.

See,{ac} O Yahweh, for I am in distress;
my inner parts churn;
my heart is turned over within me,
for I have been very rebellious.
Outside the sword bereaves;
inside, it is like death.

They heard{ad} that I groan,
with none to comfort me;
all my enemies heard of my trouble;
they rejoice that you have done it.
Bring on the day you have proclaimed,
and let them be as I am.

May every{ae} wicked deed of theirs come into your view;
treat them harshly,
as harshly as you treated me
because of every rebellion of mine;
for many are my sighs,
and sick is my heart.”

Ah, how{a} the Lord in his wrath
has wrapped daughter Zion in cloud!{b}
Out of the sky to the dust he has hurled
Israel’s glory;
his own footrest{c} he has put out of mind
in the day of his fury.

Pitilessly{d} the Lord has gulped down
every homestead of Jacob;
in his rage he has demolished
daughter Judah’s fortresses;
he has leveled them to the earth;
kingdom and rulers he has desecrated.

In fierce anger he has chopped off{e}
all Israel’s strength;
he has pulled back his right hand
when the enemy came;
he has blazed up against Jacob like a fire in flames
consuming everything near.

He has drawn{f} his bow like an enemy,
his right hand poised like an adversary;
he has killed everything pleasing to see
in the tent of Daughter Zion;
like fire, his rage has poured out.

The Lord{g} has become like an enemy;
he has swallowed up Israel;
he has swallowed up all her palaces;
he has laid waste his strongholds,
and multiplied in daughter Judah
mourning and moaning.{h}

He has wrecked{i} his booth like a garden;
he has laid waste his meeting place;
Yahweh has made Zion forget
appointed feast and sabbath,
and in the indignation of his anger
has spurned king and priest.

His altar{j} the Lord has spurned,
his holy place renounced;
into the enemy’s grip he has handed over
the ramparts of her citadels;
in Yahweh’s house they made an uproar
like the din of a festival day.

Yahweh{k} resolved to wreck
daughter Zion’s wall;
he stretched the surveyor’s cord;
his hand he did not draw back from ruin;
he set bulwark and wall to grieving;
both alike waste away.

Sunk{l} into the ground are her gates;
he has ruined and broken her bars;
her king and her princes are among the nations;
there is no instruction;{m}
her prophets also find
no vision from Yahweh.

Silent on the earth{n} sit
daughter Zion’s elders;
dust they have tossed upon their heads;
sackcloth they have wrapped round;
Jerusalem’s maidens
droop their heads to the earth.

My eyes{o} are spent with tears;
my inner parts churn;
my liver{p} is poured out on the ground
because of the ruin of the daughter of my people,
as infant and nursing child faint
in the squares of the city.

All your enemies{w} have opened{v}
their mouths wide at you;
they whistle and grind their teeth;
they cry, “We have devoured her!
This is the day we waited for;
we have lived to see it!”

Yahweh{x} has carried out his plan;
he has accomplished the decree
he ordained long ago;
he has demolished without mercy;
he has let the enemy gloat over you
and raised your adversaries’ strength.

Their heart{y} cried out to the Lord.{z}
O wall of daughter Zion,
let tears run down like a torrent
day and night!
Give yourself no respite;
let not the pupil of your eye be still!

Arise,{aa} cry out in the night,
at the beginning of the watches!{ab}
Pour out your heart like water
before the face of the Lord!
Lift up your hands to him
for the lives of your little ones,
who faint for hunger
at the head of every street.{ac}

They lie{ae} on the ground in the streets,
young and old;
my young women and my young men
have fallen by the sword;
you have slain them in the day of your anger;
you have slaughtered without pity.

You summoned,{af} as to a day of appointed feast,
my terrors from all around;
and on the day of Yahweh’s anger
none escaped or survived;
those whom I cherished and reared
my enemy has destroyed.”

Even jackals{c} bare the breast
and suckle their young;
but the daughter of my people has turned cruel,
like the ostriches in the wilderness.{d}

Her princes{i} were brighter than snow,{j}
whiter than milk;
their bodies were ruddier than coral,
their form like lapis lazuli.

Blacker{k} than charcoal their faces have grown;
in the lanes no one knows them;
their hide clings shrunken to their frame,
withered like a stick.

Yahweh{n} has used up his rage;
he has unleashed his fierce anger;
he lit a fire in Zion
that has devoured her foundations.

“Off with you! Defiled!”{r} they shouted at them;
“Off, off! Keep your hands away!”
So they ran and roamed about;
among the peoples men said,
“Here they may lodge no more.”

Yahweh’s own presence{s} has dispersed them;{t}
no more will he look upon them;
for priests there was no respect,
for elders no kindness.

Still{u} our eyes failed,
looking in vain for help;
from our watchtower we watched
for a nation that could not save.{v}

Our footsteps{w} they stalked,
barring us from walking in our own plazas;
near was our finish, full our days;
yes, our finish had arrived.

Swifter{x} were our pursuers
than the eagles of the heavens;
on the mountains they chased us;
in the wilderness they lay in wait for us.

Be joyful{aa} and celebrate, daughter of Edom,
you who live in the land of Uz!{ab}
The cup will come around to you too;
you will get drunk and expose your nakedness.

Your guilt,{ac} daughter of Zion, has been paid in full;
he will not keep you in exile any longer;
but your guilt, daughter of Edom, he will punish;
he will lay bare your sins.

He broke off its highest twig
and took it to a land of merchants;
he planted it in a city of traders.

Then he took some of the seed of the land
and planted it in fertile soil;
he set it like a willow beside abundant waters.{a}

She brought up one of her cubs;
he became a young lion.
He learned to tear prey;
he devoured people.

Nations got word of him;
in their pit he was taken.
With hooks they hauled him off
to Egypt’s land.{a}

He prowled among the lions;
he became a young lion.
He learned to tear prey;
he devoured people.

But in fury it was torn out
and flung to the earth.
Its fruit was parched by the east wind;
its mighty boughs broke away and dried;
fire devoured them.

sharpened for slaughter,
polished to flash like lightning!
Shall we rejoice? The scepter of my son despises every tree.{b}

so that hearts may melt
and the stumbling may be many.
At all their gates I have set the slaughter of the sword.{e}
Ah! It is made to flash like lightning;
it is grasped for slaughter.

With drunkenness and grief you will be filled;
a cup of ruin and desolation
is your sister Samaria’s cup.

Tell a parable to the rebellious house and say to them, ‘This is what the Lord Yahweh says:
Set the pot on the fire, set it on;
pour water into it as well.

Gather its pieces into it,
every choice piece, the thigh and the shoulder;
pack it full of the best bones.

Take the choicest of the flock;
pile the bones{b} under it.
Bring it to a rolling boil;
let its bones seethe in it.

“‘So the Lord Yahweh says this:
Woe to the city of blood,
the pot with corrosion{c} inside,
whose corrosion will not come out!
Take it out, piece after piece;
cast no lot for it.{d}

It has exhausted my efforts;{f}
its heavy corrosion will not leave it.
Let its corrosion go into the fire!

They made your oars
from the oaks of Bashan;
they made your benches out of ivory
set in cypress{b} from the shores of Kittim.{c}

Your sail was of Egypt’s embroidered linen,
spread out to be your ensign;
your canopy was violet and purple
from the shores of Elishah.{d}

They will shave their heads for you
and put on sackcloth;
they will weep over you in bitterness of soul,
with bitter mourning.{v}

All who live on the coasts
are shocked by you;
their kings bristle with fear;
their faces are twisted.

The merchants among the peoples hiss at you;
you have become a terror,
and you will be no more forever.’”

“‘The Lord Yahweh says this:
I will wipe out the idols
and make an end of Memphis’s worthless gods.{d}
No prince will rise again
from Egypt’s land,
and I will put fear in Egypt’s land.

I will set Egypt ablaze;
Pelusium will writhe in pain,
Thebes will be split open,
and Memphis will meet foes in broad day.{h}

On’s{i} youths and those of Pi-beseth{j}
will be cut down by the sword,
and the towns themselves{k} will go into captivity.

He changes times and seasons;
he removes kings and sets up kings;
he gives wisdom to the wise
and knowledge to those who have understanding.

He uncovers deep and secret matters;
he knows what is hidden in darkness,
and light lives with him.

I issue a decree that throughout all the dominion of my kingdom people shall tremble and fear before Daniel’s God,
for he is the living God,
and he endures forever;
his kingdom is one that will not be destroyed,
and his rule will last to the end.

He will say hostile words against the Most High
and wear out the holy ones who belong to the Most High;
he will intend to change the seasons and the law,
and for a time, times and half a time
the holy ones will be under his control.{g}

And many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake,
some to everlasting life,
and some to disgrace and everlasting contempt.{a}

“On that day,” announces Yahweh,
“you will address me as ‘My husband,’
and you will no more say ‘My Baal.’{f}

I will take you as my bride for all time;
I will take you as my bride with righteousness and justice,
with faithful love and mercy.

“On that day I will answer,” declares Yahweh,
“I will answer the heavens,
and they will answer the earth;

I will sow her for myself in the land;
on Lo-ruhamah I will have mercy,
and to Lo-ammi I will say, ‘You are my people’;
and he shall say, ‘My God.’ ”{h}

Yet let no one bring a charge,
let no one rebuke;
for with you is my contention, O priest.{b}

They will eat but never be full;
they will be promiscuous but never increase;
because they have deserted Yahweh
to give themselves to

My people ask advice of their wooden thing,
and their rod answers them;
for a spirit of harlotry has misled them,
and they have played the harlot out from under their God.

I will not punish your daughters for their harlotry,
or your daughters-in-law for their adulteries;
for the men themselves go off with harlots
and offer sacrifices with the shrine women;{e}
so a people that does not discern is thrown down.

Though you, Israel, play the harlot,
do not let Judah incur guilt.
Do not come to Gilgal;
do not go up to Beth-aven;{f}
do not swear, “As Yahweh lives.”

Hear this, you priests!
Pay heed, house of Israel!
Give ear, house of the king!
For the judgment concerns you;
for you have been a snare at Mizpah,
and a net spread out upon Tabor.

I know Ephraim,{b}
and Israel is not hidden from me;
for now, Ephraim, you have played the harlot;
Israel is defiled.

Israel’s arrogance testifies against him;
Israel and Ephraim will fall through their guilt;
Judah too will fall with them.

With their flocks and their herds they shall go
to seek Yahweh, but they shall not find him;
he has withdrawn from them.

Toward Yahweh they have been treacherous,
for they have fathered children of strangers.
Now a new moon will eat them up, them and their portions.{c}

The princes of Judah have become
like those who shift the boundary stone;
on them I will pour out my wrath like water.

I will return to my home
until they admit their guilt and look for me;
in their misery they will seek me eagerly.

“Come, let us go back to Yahweh;
he has ripped us apart, but he will heal us;
he has wounded us, but he will bandage us.

After two days he will revive us;
on the third day he will raise us up,{a}
that we may live before him.

Let us know, let us press on to know Yahweh;
his going out is as certain as the dawn;
he will come to us like the showers,
like the spring rain that waters the earth.”

Therefore I have hewn them by the prophets;
I have slain them by the words of my mouth,
and my judgment goes out like light.{b}

As robbers lie in wait for a man,
so a band of priests murders on the road to Shechem;
indeed they commit villainy.

When I would bring healing to Israel,
then Ephraim’s guilt comes to light,
and Samaria’s wicked acts;
for they deal in falsehood;
thieves break in,
and raiders plunder in the streets.

For they draw near; their heart is like an oven in their ambush;
all night their anger{a} sleeps;
in the morning it blazes like a flaming fire.

Every one of them is hot like an oven;
they consume those who judge them.{b}
Their kings fall, one after another,
and not one among them calls to me.

The pride of Israel testifies to his face;
yet they do not return to Yahweh their God,
nor seek him for all this.

Ephraim has become like a dove,
gullible and senseless;
to Egypt they call, to Assyria they go.

Wherever they go I will cast my net upon them;
like birds of the sky I will pull them down;
I will chasten them as their congregation has heard.{c}

They do not call to me with their heart
when they howl on their beds;
for the sake of grain and wine they slash themselves;{d}
they turn their backs on me.

Your calf is rejected, Samaria;{b}
my anger burns against them.
How long will they be incapable of innocence?

For they sow the wind,
and they shall reap the whirlwind.
The standing grain has no head;
it shall yield no flour;
and if it should yield,
strangers would devour it.

Israel is swallowed up;
already they are among the nations
like a vessel no one wants.

For they have gone to Assyria,
like a wild donkey off by itself;
Ephraim has paid for lovers.

As for my sacrificial gifts,
they sacrifice meat and eat it;
Yahweh does not accept them.
Now he will remember their iniquity
and punish their sins;
they shall return to Egypt.

They will not stay on Yahweh’s land;
Ephraim will go back to Egypt,
and eat what is unclean in Assyria.

They will pour out no wine for Yahweh,
and their sacrifices will not please him.
Their meals will resemble funeral food;
anyone who eats it will be defiled;
their bread will only satisfy their hunger;
it will not enter Yahweh’s house.

For look, they have gone, fleeing from ruin;
Egypt will gather them up,
Memphis{a} will bury them.
Their treasures of silver will be overrun by nettles;
brambles will grow in their tents.

Ephraim is a watchman with my God—the prophet;
yet a bird-catcher’s trap lies on all his paths,
and enmity in the house of his God.

They have deeply corrupted themselves
as in the days of Gibeah;{b}
he will remember their iniquity;
he will punish their sins.

All their evil is at Gilgal;{e}
there I came to hate them.
Because of the wickedness of their deeds
I will drive them out of my house.
I will love them no more;
all their princes are rebels.

Ephraim is stricken;
their root is dried up;
they shall bear no fruit.
Even though they give birth,
I will put to death their cherished offspring.

My God will cast them off,
for they have not obeyed him,
and among the nations they will be wanderers.

Israel is a rambling vine{a}
that puts forth fruit for itself.
As his fruit grew plentiful,
so he added altars;
as his land grew good,
so he made his pillars handsome.

They speak mere words;
with empty oaths they cut covenants;
so judgment springs up like poisonous weeds
in the furrows of the field.

Since the days of Gibeah you have sinned, Israel;
there they have stood fast.{i}
Will not war overtake them in Gibeah,
against the children of injustice?

When I please, I will chasten them,
and peoples will be gathered against them
when they are bound for their double iniquity.{j}

Ephraim was a heifer well taught,
fond of threshing,
and I passed over her fair neck;{k}
but now I will harness Ephraim;
Judah shall drag the plow;
Jacob shall break the clods for himself.

Plant righteousness for yourselves;
harvest the fruit of faithful love;
plow your fallow ground,
for it is time to seek Yahweh,
until he comes
and showers righteousness on you.{l}

You have plowed wickedness;
you have reaped injustice;
you have eaten falsehood’s fruit.
Since you relied on your own way,{m}
on your multitude of warriors,

But I myself taught Ephraim to walk,
lifting them in my{d} arms;
yet they never knew that it was I who healed them.

I drew them with human ties,
with bonds of love;
I was like someone who lifts the yoke from their necks,{e}
and I stooped down and fed them.

They will not{f} go back to Egypt;
Assyria will rule them,
because they refuse to come back to me.

The sword will whirl through their cities;
it will make an end of their gate bars{g}
and devour them because of their schemes.

My people are bent on turning from me;
though they call to the Most High,{h}
he will by no means raise them up.

How can I give you up, Ephraim?
How can I hand you over, Israel?
How can I make you like Admah?
How can I treat you like Zeboiim?{i}
My heart turns over within me;
all my compassion grows warm and tender.

I will not act on my burning anger;
I will not turn back to destroy Ephraim;
for I am God and not a man,
the Holy One in your midst,
and I will not come in wrath.{j}

They will walk after Yahweh;
he will roar like a lion;
when he roars,
his children will come trembling from the west.

Like birds they will come trembling out of Egypt,
and like doves from the land of Assyria;
and I will settle them in their homes, declares Yahweh.

Ephraim has surrounded me with lies,{k}
and the house of Israel with deceit;
but Judah still wanders with God,{l}
and is faithful to the Holy One.{m}

Ephraim feeds on wind,{a}
and chases the scorching east wind all day;
he piles up lies and violence;
they sign a treaty with Assyria,
and olive oil is shipped to Egypt.

Yahweh has a dispute with Judah too;
he will punish Jacob for his conduct
and pay him back for what he has done.

He strove with the angel and prevailed;
he wept and sought his favor.
At Bethel he found him,{c}
and there he spoke with us{d}—

As for you, return to your God;
keep steadfast love and justice,
and wait on your God continually.

Ephraim boasts, “I have become rich;
I have acquired wealth for myself;
nothing they find in all my work
will count as sin against me.”

I am Yahweh your God
since the land of Egypt;
once more I will make you dwell in tents,
as in the days of the festival.{h}

I spoke through the prophets;
I gave vision upon vision,
and through the prophets I spoke in likenesses.

If Gilead is wickedness,
they are surely worthless;
in Gilgal they sacrifice bulls;
their altars also will be like stone heaps
on the furrows of the field.{i}

Jacob fled to the open country of Aram;{j}
there Israel served for a wife,
and for a wife he kept sheep.

Ephraim has given bitter provocation;
therefore his Lord will let his blood remain upon him
and pay him back for his contempt.

Once when Ephraim spoke, men trembled;
he was lifted high in Israel;
but he became guilty through Baal, and he died.

Now they sin more and more;
they make themselves cast images,
idols from their silver according to their skill,
every one a craftsman’s product.
Of these they say,
“Men who sacrifice kiss calves!”{a}

But I am Yahweh your God
from the land of Egypt;
you know no God but me,
and besides me there is no savior.{c}

As they grazed, they were sated;
sated, their hearts grew proud;
and so they forgot me.

Like a bear bereft of her young I will meet them
and rip apart the covering of their heart;{e}
there like a lioness I will consume them;
the beast of the field shall tear them.

Shall I ransom them from the power of Sheol?{h}
Shall I redeem them from Death?
O Death, where are your plagues?
O Sheol, where is your sting?{i}
Compassion is hidden from my eyes.

Samaria will bear her guilt,{k}
because she has rebelled against her God;
they will fall by the sword;
their little ones will be dashed in pieces,
and their pregnant women ripped open.

Assyria will not save us;
we will not ride on horses;
and we will no more say ‘Our God’
to the work of our hands.
In you the orphan finds mercy.”

I will heal their faithlessness;
I will love them freely,
for my anger has turned away from them.

I will be like the dew to Israel;
he will blossom like the lily
and strike root like the trees of Lebanon.{c}

His shoots will spread;
his splendor will be like the olive,
and his fragrance like Lebanon.

Those who live in his shade will return;
they will grow grain{d}
and blossom like the vine;
their renown will be like the wine of Lebanon.

Ephraim, what have I to do with idols anymore?{e}
I answer him, I look after him.
I am like an evergreen cypress;
your fruit is found from me.

What the cutter left, the swarmer has eaten;
what the swarmer left, the hopper has eaten;
and what the hopper left, the stripper has eaten.{b}

It has turned my vine into a waste
and splintered my fig tree;
it has peeled it bare and thrown it down;
its branches are bleached white.

The fields are ruined;
the ground mourns;
for the grain is destroyed,
the new wine dries up,
the oil fails.

Hang your heads, you tillers of the soil;
howl, you who tend the vines,
for wheat and for barley,
since the field’s harvest is lost.

The vine has withered;
the fig tree droops;
pomegranate, palm, and apple,{d}
every tree of the field is dried up;
and gladness has withered away
from the children of mankind.

Alas for the day!
For the day of Yahweh is near,
and as destruction from the Almighty it comes.{e}

The seeds shrivel beneath the clods;{f}
the storehouses are desolate;
the granaries are torn down,
for the grain has dried up.

How the beasts groan!
The herds of cattle wander in confusion
because they have no pasture;
even the flocks of sheep are made to suffer.{g}

Like mighty men they run;
like men of war they climb the wall.
They march each in his own line;
they do not break their ranks.

Upon the city they spring;
along the wall they run;
into the houses they climb;
in through the windows they come like a thief.

Yahweh utters his voice
before his army,
for his host is exceedingly great;
mighty is he who carries out his word.
For the day of Yahweh is great and very fearsome;
who can endure it?

Rend your heart and not your garments,
and turn back to Yahweh your God;
for he is gracious and merciful,
slow to anger and great in steadfast love,
and he repents of the harm.{d}

Blow the horn in Zion;
set apart a fast;
call a solemn assembly;

Do not be afraid, O land;
be glad and rejoice,
for Yahweh has done great things!

Do not fear, wild animals,
for the desert pastures are turning green;
trees are bearing fruit;
fig and vine are producing richly.

“And afterward{i}
I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh;
your sons and your daughters will prophesy;
your old men will dream dreams,
and your young men will see visions.

I will assemble all the nations
and lead them down into the Valley of Jehoshaphat.{b}
There I will go to law with them
for my people and my inheritance Israel,
whom they dispersed among the nations;
and they parceled out my land.

Proclaim this among the nations:
Prepare for war;{f}
stir up the warriors.
Let all the fighting men draw near;
let them come up.

Hammer your plowshares into swords
and your pruning hooks into spears;{g}
let the weakling say, “I am a warrior.”

I will avenge their blood,
which I have not yet avenged;{j}
for Yahweh dwells in Zion.”

This is what Yahweh says:
“For three transgressions of Moab,
yes, for four, I will not turn it back;
because he burned the bones of the king of Edom
to lime.{a}

From among them I will cut off the judge{c}
and slay all her princes along with him,”
says Yahweh.

They trample the heads of the poor
into the dirt{e}
and shove the humble aside;
a man and his father visit the same girl,
and so profane my holy name;

Look, I am going to make you groan where you are,{h}
as a wagon groans
that is loaded with sheaves.

Speed will not help the swift escape;
the strong will not summon their power,
and the warrior will not rescue his life.

The one who handles the bow will not hold firm;
the fleet-footed will not get away,
and the one who rides the horse will not save his life.

“You alone have I known{a}
of all the families of the earth;
therefore I will punish you
for all your iniquities.”

Surely the Lord Yahweh will not do a thing
unless he has disclosed his counsel{c}
to his servants the prophets.

The lion has roared;
who will not fear?
The Lord Yahweh has spoken;
who can but prophesy?

“Enter Bethel and rebel;
enter Gilgal and rebel more;
bring sacrifices at dawn each day,
and your tithes every third day;{d}

“I also held back the rain from you
while there were three months yet until harvest;
on one town I made it rain,
and on another town I made it not rain;
one plot was rained on,
and the plot that got no rain dried up.

“I struck you with scorching and mildew;
the locust ate your gardens and your vineyards,
your figs and your olives, many as they were;
but you have not come back to me,”
declares Yahweh.

“I sent pestilence on you in the way of Egypt;
with the sword I slew your young men,
and your horses were taken captive,
and I made the stink of your camps go up even into your nostrils;
but you have not come back to me,”
declares Yahweh.

“Therefore this is what I will do to you, Israel;
because I will do this to you,
prepare to meet your God, Israel!”

Do not go seeking Bethel;
do not enter Gilgal;
do not pass over to Beersheba;
for Gilgal shall go off into exile,{b}
and Bethel shall become nothing.”{c}

Now you shall carry off Sakkuth your king
and Kaiwan, your star god,{j} your idols
that you fashioned for yourselves;

Cross over to Calneh and see;
go from there to Hamath the great;
then go down to Gath of the Philistines.
Are you better than these kingdoms?
Or is their territory greater than your territory?{a}

you who rejoice in Lo-debar,{f}
who say, “Was it not by our own strength
that we took Karnaim for ourselves?”

“In that day,” declares the Lord Yahweh,
“I will bring the sun down at midday
and cover the earth with darkness while it is still day.{e}

I will convert your festivals into funerals
and all your songs into laments;
I will make everyone wear sackcloth
and shave every head;
I will make it a time of grieving, as for an only son,
and its finish will be a bitter day.

I saw the Lord standing by{a} the altar, and he said:
“Strike the tops of the pillars so the doorframes tremble,
and smash them down on everyone’s heads;
the survivors I will slaughter with the sword.
No one will get away;
no one will escape.

The Lord Yahweh of Armies{b}—
at his touch the earth melts,
and all its inhabitants mourn;
the whole of it rises like the Nile
and falls back like the river of Egypt;

so that they may take over what is left of Edom
and every nation that bears my name,”{g}
announces Yahweh, who will do this.

“See, days are coming,” announces Yahweh,
“when the plowman will catch up with the harvester,
and the grape stomper with the one who sows seed;
the mountains will trickle with new wine,
and all the hills will run with it.{h}

I will bring back the captivity{i} of my people Israel;
they shall build the wasted cities and dwell in them;
they shall plant vineyards and drink the wine from them,
and lay out gardens and eat the fruit from them.

Your confederates have pushed you to the frontier;
your friends have tricked you and prevailed against you;
your table companions lay a snare{c} under you—
he has no discernment.

On that day, declares Yahweh,
will I not destroy the wise men out of Edom,
and understanding out of the mount of Esau?{d}

Your warriors will be dismayed, Teman,{e}
so that every man will be cut off from the mount of Esau
by slaughter.

You should not have entered my people’s gate
in the day of their calamity;
you, of all people, should not have gazed on his misery
in the day of his calamity;
you should not have reached for his goods
in the day of his calamity.{g}

For as you have drunk on my holy mountain,{i}
so all the nations will drink continually;
they will drink and swallow,
and will be as though they had never been.

But on Mount Zion there will be those who escape,
and it will be holy,
and the house of Jacob will possess its own possessions.{j}

The exiles of this army of Israel’s children
shall own Canaanite land up to Zarephath,{m}
and Jerusalem’s exiles in Sepharad{n}
shall have the towns of the Negev.

He said,
“In my distress I called out to Yahweh,
and he responded;
from the belly of Sheol{b} I screamed for help,
and you listened to my voice.

And I said, ‘I am driven away
from before your eyes;
yet I will look again{c}
toward your holy temple.’

The waters closed over me up to the throat;{d}
the deep was all around me;
seaweed wrapped itself around my head.

To the roots of the mountains I went down;
the earth with its bars shut behind me forever;
yet you brought my life up from the pit,
Yahweh my God.

Listen, you peoples, all of you;
pay attention, earth, and all that fills it;
and let the Lord Yahweh be witness against you,
the Lord from his holy temple.

All her carved idols will be smashed to bits,
all her earnings{e} burned with fire,
and all her images I will make a desolation;
for from a prostitute’s fee she gathered them,
and to a prostitute’s fee they will return.”

Because of this I will mourn and howl;
I will walk around barefoot and stripped;
I will cry like the jackals
and moan like the owls.

Her wound is incurable;
it has spread to Judah;
it has arrived at my people’s gate,
at Jerusalem itself.

Tell it not in Gath;{f}
weep not at all.
In Beth-leaphrah{g}
roll yourself in the dust.

Pass on your way,
inhabitants of Shaphir,{h} in shameful nakedness;
the inhabitants of Zaanan{i} do not come out;
Beth-ezel is wailing,
it will take away from you its support.{j}

So you will give parting gifts{m}
to Moresheth-gath;
the houses of Achzib{n} will be a deception
to Israel’s kings.

Again I will bring a conqueror{o} upon you,
inhabitants of Mareshah;
the glory of Israel
will come to Adullam.{p}

“Stop your preaching,” so they preach.{b}
“No one should preach about these things;
shame will never catch up with us.”

“I will surely gather every one of you, Jacob;
I will assemble the survivors of Israel;
I will crowd them together like sheep in a fold,{c}
like livestock in their meadow;
the place will buzz with crowds.

You despise good and adore evil;
you peel the hide from my people{a}
and the meat from their bones,

So night will fall on you, and no vision come;
dark will it be for you, and no divining;
the sun will set upon the prophets,
and the daylight turn black above them.

Shamed will be the seers
and abashed the diviners;
all of them will cover their lips,{b}
for God gives no answer.

He will arbitrate among many peoples
and settle quarrels among powerful distant nations;
they will beat their swords into plowshares
and their spears into pruning hooks;
nation will not lift a sword at nation,
and they will no longer learn how to fight.

Writhe and groan,{f} Daughter Zion,
like a woman giving birth;
for now you must go out from the town
and lodge in the countryside;
you will go to Babylon.
There you will be rescued;
there Yahweh will redeem you
from the grip of your enemies.

Rise up and thresh, Daughter Zion,
for I will make your horn iron
and your hoofs bronze;
you will crush many peoples,
and devote their gain to Yahweh,{g}
their wealth to the Lord of all the earth.

Now muster your troops, daughter of troops;{a}
a siege is laid against us;
with a rod they strike on the cheek
the judge of Israel.

But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah,{b}
small among Judah’s clans,
from you will come out for me
the future ruler of Israel,
whose roots reach back
to ancient times.{c}

And he shall be our peace.{d}
When Assyria invades our land
and marches over our citadels,
we will set up seven shepherds against it,
yes, eight princes of the people.{e}

“In that day,” declares Yahweh,
“I will cut off your horses from among you
and destroy your chariots;

Hear, you mountains, Yahweh’s lawsuit,{a}
you enduring foundations of the earth;
for Yahweh has a case against his people,
and with Israel he will argue it.

My people, recall now
what Balak, Moab’s king, schemed,
what reply Balaam son of Beor gave him,{b}
and all that took place between Shittim and Gilgal,{c}
that you may know the righteous acts{d} of Yahweh.”

He has shown you, human being,{e} what is good;
what does Yahweh require of you
except to act justly, to cherish loyal love,{f}
and to live humbly{g} with your God?

Yahweh’s voice cries out to the city—
and it is wise to revere your name:{h}
“Hear this, tribe, and the one who assigned it!{i}

Her rich are full of violence;
her inhabitants speak lies,
and their tongue is deceit in their mouths.

You will plant but never harvest;
you will press olives but never rub on the oil,
and crush grapes but never drink wine.

For you have kept the statutes of Omri{n}
and all the practices of Ahab’s house,
and you have walked in their counsels;
so I will turn you into a wasteland
and your{o} people into something to hiss at;
you will bear the scorn of my people.”

The faithful have perished from the land,
and there is no one upright among humankind;
all of them lie in ambush for blood;
each hunts his brother with a net.

Do not rely on a companion;
do not lean on a close friend;
even from her who lies in your arms
keep watch over the door of your lips.

For the son treats his father with contempt,
the daughter rises against her mother,
the daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law;
a man’s enemies are those of his own house.{b}

But as for me, I will watch for Yahweh;
I will wait for the God of my salvation;
my God will hear me.

On that day they will come to you
from Assyria and the cities of Egypt,{d}
and from Egypt to the River,{e}
from sea to sea and from mountain to mountain.

The nations will see and be ashamed
of all their might;
they will lay their hands on their mouths;
their ears will be deaf.

They will lick the dust like serpents,
like creatures that crawl on the ground;
they will shake as they leave their fortresses;
they will turn in terror to Yahweh our God,
and they will fear you.

Yahweh is kind,
a refuge when trouble comes;
he cares for those who shelter in him.

But with an overflowing flood
he will make a full end of her place,{c}
and darkness shall pursue his enemies.

What do you devise against Yahweh?
He will make a full end;
distress shall not rise up a second time.

For though they are tangled like thorns
and drunk as with their drink,
they shall be devoured like stubble fully dried.{d}

From you has gone out one
who devises evil against Yahweh,
a counselor of worthlessness.{e}

Behold, upon the mountains the feet of him who brings good news,
who proclaims peace!{f}
Celebrate your feasts, O Judah;
pay your vows;
for the worthless one shall not again pass through you;
he is cut off completely.

One who scatters has come up against your face.{a}
Guard the rampart;
watch the road;
brace your loins;
gather all your strength.

The shield of his mighty men is made red;
the warriors are clothed in scarlet;
the chariots gleam with the fire of steel
on the day he prepares for battle,
and the spears of cypress are brandished.{b}

The chariots rage in the streets;
they rush to and fro in the squares;
their appearance is like torches;
they dart about like the lightning.

He calls to mind his nobles;
they stumble as they march;
they hurry to her wall,
and the siege shield is set up.

It is decided: she is stripped; she is carried off,{c}
and her maidservants moan like the sound of doves,
beating upon their breasts.

“I am your enemy,” announces Yahweh of Armies,{e}
“and I will set your chariots ablaze,{f}
the sword will eat up your young lions,
I will remove your prey from the land,
and the cry of your envoys will never again be heard.”

Woe to the city of blood,
all of it full of lies and plunder;
the prey never departs!

And it shall come to pass that all who look on you shall shrink from you and say,
‘Nineveh is laid waste; who will grieve for her?’
Where shall I seek comforters for you?”{a}

Are you better than No-amon,{b}
that sat by the Nile channels,
with water all around her,
whose rampart was the sea,
and water her wall?{c}

Yet she too went into exile;
she went into captivity;
her little ones also were dashed to pieces
at the head of every street;
they cast lots for her honored men,
and all her great men were bound in chains.

You also shall be drunk;
you shall go into hiding;
you also shall seek a refuge from the enemy.

All your strongholds are fig trees with the firstfruits:
if they are shaken,
they fall into the mouth of the eater.

Behold, your troops in your midst are women.
The gates of your land are thrown wide open to your enemies;
fire has devoured your bars.

Draw water for the siege;
strengthen your fortresses;
go into the clay and tread the mortar;
take hold of the brick mold!

There the fire shall devour you;
the sword shall cut you off;
it shall devour you as the young locust devours.
Multiply yourself like the young locust;
multiply yourself like the locust swarm!{e}

You have multiplied your merchants
more than the stars of the heavens;
the young locust strips and flies away.

Your guardsmen are like the locust swarm,
and your officials like clouds of grasshoppers
that settle in the walls on a cold day;
when the sun rises, they flee,
and no one knows the place where they are.{f}

So the law grows numb,
and justice never goes forth;
for the wicked hem in the righteous;
so justice goes forth twisted.

Its horses are swifter than leopards,
and keener than wolves at evening.
Its horsemen gallop proudly;
its horsemen come from far away;
they fly like an eagle swooping to devour.

They all come for violence;
the set of their faces is forward;{d}
they gather captives like sand.

Are you not eternal,
Yahweh my God, my Holy One?
We will not perish.{f}
Yahweh, you have set them in place to punish us;
Rock, you have established them to correct us.

He lifts them all up with the angle;
he hauls them away in his net
and gathers them in his dragnet;
therefore he rejoices and exults.

Upon my watch I will stand,
and set myself upon the tower;
I will look out to see what he will speak with me,
and what reply I shall make to my complaint.{a}

And Yahweh answered me and said:
“Write the vision;
make it plain upon tablets,
so that one may run who reads it.{b}

For the vision is still for the appointed time;
it hastens toward the end, and it will not lie.
If it seems slow, wait for it;
for it will surely come; it will not delay.{c}

“Behold, the one who is puffed up,
his soul is not upright within him;
but the righteous shall live by his faithfulness.{d}

“Wine, moreover, is a traitor;{e}
the proud man never rests at home.
His appetite gapes wide as Sheol,{f}
and like death he is never full.
He rakes in every nation
and gathers up all peoples.”

You have devised shame for your house
by cutting off many peoples;
you have sinned against your own soul.

Behold, is it not from Yahweh of Armies{g}
that peoples toil only for the fire,
and nations weary themselves for nothing?

For the earth shall be filled
with the knowledge of the glory of Yahweh,
as the waters cover the sea.{h}

Woe to him who makes his neighbors drink,
pouring out your wrath and making them drunk,
in order to look upon their nakedness!{i}

The violence you did to Lebanon will overwhelm you,
and the slaughter of its animals will terrify you,{k}
for human bloodshed and violence to the land,
to cities and everyone who lives in them.

Yahweh, I have heard of your fame,
and I am awestruck.
Yahweh, renew your deeds in our own time;
make them known in these years;
in your anger, remember compassion.{b}

His shining was as the light of day;
rays came forth from his hand,{d}
and there was the hiding of his power.

He stood and measured the earth;{f}
he looked and made the nations tremble.
The everlasting mountains were shattered;
the ancient hills sank low.
His goings are as of old.

Your bow was laid bare;
the arrows were sworn by your word.{h} Selah
You split the earth with rivers.

The mountains saw you and writhed;
the torrent of waters swept by;{i}
the deep uttered its voice
and raised its hands on high.

With his own spears you stabbed the heads of his warriors,{j}
who rushed out like a storm to scatter me,
gloating as though devouring the helpless in hiding.

I heard, and my body trembled;
my lips quivered at the sound;
rottenness entered into my bones;
I trembled where I stood,
because I must wait quietly for the day of distress
to come upon the people who invade us.

Though the fig tree does not blossom,
and no grapes grow on the vines;
though the olive harvest fails,
and the fields yield no grain;
though the sheep vanish from the pen,
and no cattle stand in the barns,

Yahweh, the Lord, is my strength;{k}
he makes my feet like the feet of deer,
and makes me tread upon my high places.
For the choir director: on my stringed instruments.{l}

“I will raise my hand against Judah
and all who live in Jerusalem;
from this place I will eliminate every trace of Baal,
the names of the pagan priests along with the temple priests,{c}

those who worship the stars of heaven
from their rooftops;
those who kneel and swear loyalty to Yahweh
while also swearing by Milcom;{d}

Howl, you who dwell in the Mortar!{g}
For all the people of Canaan are brought to silence;
all who weigh out silver are cut off.

Seek Yahweh, all you humble of the land,
who do his justice;
seek righteousness; seek humility;
perhaps you will be hidden
on the day of the anger of Yahweh.{b}

For Gaza shall be forsaken,
and Ashkelon a desolation;
Ashdod they shall drive out at noon,
and Ekron shall be uprooted.{c}

The coast shall belong to the remnant of the house of Judah;
on it they shall graze;
in the houses of Ashkelon
they shall lie down at evening.
For Yahweh their God will visit them
and restore their fortunes.

She has not obeyed his call;
she has refused discipline.
She has not put her trust in Yahweh;
she has not come close to her God.

Her officials within her are roaring lions;
her judges are wolves of the evening
that leave nothing until the morning.

Her prophets are reckless,
men of treachery;
her priests have profaned what is holy;
they have done violence to the law.

Yahweh within her is righteous;
he does no injustice;
morning by morning he brings his justice to light;
he does not fail;
but the unjust knows no shame.

“I have destroyed nations;
their watchtowers lie in ruins;
I have emptied their streets
so that nobody walks there;
their cities are devastated,
with no people, no inhabitants.

“For then I will turn to the peoples a purified lip,{c}
that they may all call upon Yahweh’s name
and serve him with one shoulder.{d}

From beyond the rivers of Cush
my worshipers, the daughter of my scattered ones,
shall bring my offering.{e}

The remnant of Israel shall do no injustice
and speak no lies,
nor shall a deceitful tongue be found in their mouth;
for they shall graze and lie down,
and none shall make them afraid.”

Yahweh has removed your judgments;
he has turned back your enemy.
The King of Israel, Yahweh, is in your midst;
you shall never again fear evil.

On that day Jerusalem will be told:
“Zion, do not be afraid;
do not let your hands go limp.

Yahweh your God lives among you,
a warrior who gives victory;
he will take delight in you,
renew you by his love,{f}
and celebrate over you with songs.”

“I will gather those who grieve for the appointed feasts;
they were from you,
a burden of reproach upon her.{g}

An oracle.{a}
The word of Yahweh is against the land of Hadrach,
and Damascus is its resting place.
For to Yahweh is the eye of mankind
and of all the tribes of Israel;{b}

And Tyre built a fortress for herself,{c}
and piled up silver as the dust,
and gold as the mire of the streets.

And I will remove his blood from his mouth,
and his detestable things from between his teeth,
and he too will be left over for our God;
he will be as a chief in Judah,{e}
and Ekron like the Jebusite.

Celebrate loudly, Zion’s daughter!
Call out joyfully, Jerusalem’s daughter!
See, your king comes to you;
he is just and brings deliverance,{f}
humble, mounted on a donkey,
on a young donkey, a donkey’s foal.{g}

I will take away the chariot from Ephraim
and the warhorse from Jerusalem;
the warrior’s bow will be snapped,
and he will proclaim peace to the nations;
his dominion will stretch from sea to sea,
and from the Euphrates to earth’s farthest edges.{h}

For I have bent Judah as my bow;
I have filled the bow with Ephraim.
I will stir up your sons, O Zion,
against your sons, O Javan,{i}
and wield you like a warrior’s sword.

Then Yahweh will appear over them,
and his arrow will go forth like lightning;
the Lord Yahweh will blow the ram’s horn{j}
and will march in the storms of the south.

Yahweh of Armies will be a shield over them;
they will devour and will tread upon the stones of the sling;{k}
they will drink, they will roar as with wine,
and they will be filled like the basin,
like the corners of the altar.

And Yahweh their God will save them in that day
like a flock, his people;
for they are stones of a crown,
sparkling over his land.{l}

For the teraphim have spoken iniquity,{a}
and the diviners have seen a lie,
and the dreams speak falsehood;
they comfort with vapor.
Therefore they have wandered off like a flock;
they are oppressed, for there is no shepherd.

“My anger is hot against the shepherds,
and I will punish the leaders;{b}
for Yahweh of Armies cares for his flock, the house of Judah,
and will make them like his majestic steed in battle.

From him shall come the cornerstone,
from him the tent peg,
from him the battle bow,
from him every ruler—all of them together.

I will strengthen Judah’s house,
and rescue Joseph’s house,
and bring them back to live here, because I feel compassion for them;
it will be as though I had never rejected them;
for I am Yahweh their God, and I will respond to them.

And Ephraim will be like a warrior,
and their heart will be glad as with wine;
and their sons will see and be glad;
their heart will exult in Yahweh.

I will hiss for them and gather them,
for I have ransomed them;
and they will increase as they increased before.

And I will sow them among the peoples,
and in distant places they will remember me;
and they will live with their sons and will turn back.

“Blessed is God, the ever-living,
and blessed his reign!
For he chastises, and he shows mercy;
he brings down to Hades, to the lowest depths of the earth,
and he himself brings up from the great destruction;
there is nothing that can slip from his hand.

Here, in the country of my captivity, I praise him;
I proclaim his might and his majesty to a sinful people.{c}
Return, sinners, and act righteously in his sight;
who can tell? He may take pleasure in you and deal kindly with you.

I extol my God;
my soul extols heaven’s King
and will exult in his majesty.

A brilliant light will blaze to all the earth’s limits;
from afar a multitude of nations will come to you,
and the dwellers at the world’s remotest edges to your holy name,
carrying gifts in their hands for heaven’s King.
Age upon age will give exultation within you,
and the name of the elect city will last for every age to come.

Fortunate as well are all who mourned for you
because of every blow you bore;
for in you they will find joy,
and they will look on the fullness of your gladness forever.
Bless the Lord, my soul, the mighty King!

Judith said:
Lead off for my God with tambourines,
sing to the Lord with cymbals;
weave for him a psalm and a song of praise;
exalt him, and call upon his name.

For the Lord is a God who crushes wars;{a}
he set his camp in the midst of his people
and snatched me from the hand of my pursuers.

Then my lowly ones shouted for joy,
and my weak ones cried aloud, and the enemy cowered;{d}
they lifted their voices, and the enemy was routed.

Sons of slave girls pierced them through;
they wounded them like the children of deserters.
They perished before the battle line of my Lord.

For every sacrifice as a fragrant aroma is a small thing,
and all the fat for burnt offerings to you a very little thing;
but the one who reveres the Lord stands great for all time.

Love justice, you rulers of the earth;
think of the Lord{a} with goodness,
and search for him with a sincere heart,

For wisdom is a spirit that cherishes humanity,
yet she will not let a blasphemer go unpunished for what he says;{c}
for God observes his inmost feelings,{d}
watches his heart truly,
and listens to his tongue.

For the schemes of the ungodly will be investigated,
and news of what they say will reach the Lord,
to convict them of their lawless acts;

So protect yourselves from pointless complaining,
and keep your tongues from slander;
for no whispered word is without consequence,{f}
and a deceitful mouth kills the soul.

For we were born by sheer accident,
and after this we will be as if we had never existed;
the breath in our nostrils is a puff of smoke,
and thought is a spark struck by our pulsing heart;

Let none of us go without his share in our revelry;
everywhere let us leave tokens of our merriment,
because this is our portion, and this our lot.

Let us oppress the poor man who is just;
let us not spare the widow,
nor respect the gray hairs of the aged, many in years.

